2.2.5
Two self-description gaps, both found by asking a repo a question it had no way to answer. A synced repo could not name its toolkit version: `.claude/template-manifest.json` records `lastSynced` as a commit SHA and nothing else, no synced file carries a marker (grepping `templates/` for `v2.2.x` returns zero hits), so all four measured consumers answered with an opaque hex string and resolving it needed the toolkit checkout present with its tags fetched — same class as the toolkit ↔ mcp-dev-servers version contract, "do I have the fix?" needing a version handle rather than a sha. Two additive manifest fields close it, and the halves are NOT symmetric: `lastSyncedVersion` ships CLIENT-side, written by the `sync-template` skill at step 7b from `git -C <templateRepo> describe --tags [--exact-match] <lastSynced>` over the same repo and sha the server already recorded — reproducible and checkable, but explicitly not server-authoritative, and absent on any repo synced by an older skill; `templateSyncToolsVersion` is defined, reserved, read and reported but deliberately NOT written client-side, because the client can compute a tag from a sha and cannot observe the version of the process across the MCP boundary — no `template_*` response carries one, which is the underlying complaint (the consumer found on 0.1.0 could only discover it by describing a symptom), and a user-typed number in a server-owned file is worse than an absent key since the next reader cannot tell it from an authoritative one. Both are labels and never gates: absent means unknown, never stale, existing manifests stay valid untouched with zero churn, nothing classifies or migrates on either field, a sync never fails or rolls back because a version would not resolve, and the stamp writes only into an absent-or-empty key so a future authoritative server always wins. The report gains `Toolkit:` and `Sync server:` lines so a human never opens the manifest. One measured trap is now in the skill: all four manifests store `templateRepo` as an MSYS path (`/g/git/claude-code-toolkit`) that native git.exe spawned from Python cannot resolve, so both fallbacks "fail" and the label comes out `""` on a repo whose tags are right there — `''` from a Python subprocess versus `v2.2.3` from Bash on the same sha — so the resolution runs in Bash and is handed to the stamping script. Filed upstream and NOT shipped here: `template_finalize_sync` writing both fields, and any `template_*` response reporting the server version; when they land the client stamp becomes a no-op by construction. Second gap: the toolkit did not gate itself. With no root `PROJECT_CONTEXT.md`, `run-gate.sh` printed GATE SKIP and `pre-commit-test.sh` and `gate-before-merge.sh` no-opped in the repository that DEFINES them — every toolkit PR merged through the artifact path as a no-op, and two of the three git gates could not discriminate in their own repo, which cost fixture coverage twice (the malformed-payload rows and item K's four-way matrix both needed scratch repos built by hand). The file now exists, filled in and placeholder-free, with the Test/Gate split: Test is `verify-template-consistency.sh` alone (~20s, every commit), Gate is that plus `test-hooks.sh` (~600s, merge only). The split is the point rather than an optimisation — `test-hooks.sh` builds a throwaway git repo per fixture, and collapsed into one command it would put ten minutes on every commit here, the exact trap a consumer is living in. Three new consistency assertions hold it in place (file present and placeholder-free; `**Test**` is the consistency script WITHOUT test-hooks.sh; `**Gate**` runs both), because deleting the file would otherwise restore the old silence with no other symptom. `CLAUDE.md` now names the self-reference risk it creates — a bug in `pre-commit-test.sh` can block the commit that fixes `pre-commit-test.sh`, and the documented escape is the pre-existing `<cwd>/.claude/git-guard-off` kill switch, to be reached for only after reading the block message, which is far more often a real red gate than a broken hook. The fixture re-check the split invited concludes that nothing should be simplified: the committed fixtures already build their own repos via `mkrepo`, and pointing them at the toolkit checkout would couple the suite to the very `PROJECT_CONTEXT.md` under test; what got easier is ad-hoc verification, where a hand-built scratch repo is no longer needed to watch a gate discriminate. Consistency 264 → 267; hook fixtures 342, unchanged. Downstream: re-copy `skills/sync-template/SKILL.md`; item 2 is toolkit-internal and consumers are unaffected.
